\displaystyle\left(5+4 \cdot \frac{1}{2}\right)\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\displaystyle\right)^{2}= \boxed{\frac{7}{2}}.
\][asy]
unitsize(2cm);
for (int i=0; i<4; ++i) {
for (int j=0; j<4; ++j) {
draw((i,0)--(i,3),dashed);
draw((0,j)--(3,j),dashed);
};}
draw((1,0)--(2,0)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(2,3)--(1,3)--(0,2)--(0,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
for (int i=0; i<2; ++i) {
label("1",(0.5+2i,2.5),S);
label("1",(0.5+2i,0.5),N);}
label("$\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$",(0,1.5),E);
label("$\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$",(3,1.5),W);
[/asy]

response:
Let's cut the octagon in half along one axis of symmetry, as shown below.  The right triangle with one side of length $\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and hypotenuse of length $1$ splits into a right isosceles triangle with legs of $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and a 30-60-90 triangle with catheti $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$ and $\frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}}$.

[asy]
unitsize(2 cm);

draw((0,0)--(1/sqrt(2),0)--(1,1/sqrt(2))--(1 + 1/sqrt(2),1/sqrt(2))--(1 + 1/sqrt(2),1 + 1/sqrt(2))--(1/sqrt(2) + 1,1 + 1/sqrt(2))--(1/sqrt(2) + 1,1/sqrt(2))--(0, 1/sqrt(2)));

draw((-1/sqrt(2),0)--(1 + 1/sqrt(2),1/sqrt(2)));

draw((1/sqrt(2),0)--(0.86,0.44));

draw((0,1/sqrt(2))--(0,1.16));

label("$A$", (0, 1.16), W);
label("$B$", (1, 1.16), W);
label("$C$", (1, 1/sqrt(2)), NW + NE);
label("$D$", (1 + 1/sqrt(2), 1/sqrt(2)), E);
label("$E$", (1 + 1/sqrt(2), 1 + 1/sqrt(2)), A);
label("$F$", (1/sqrt(2) + 1, 1 + 1/sqrt(2)), E);
label("$G$", (1/sqrt(2) + 1, 1/sqrt(2)), W);
label("$H$", (0,1.16), W);
[/asy]

The right triangle forms a quarter circle with radius $1$.  Its sector has central angle $\frac{3\pi}{4}$, so its area is worth $\frac{3}{16}$ of the area of the circle.  The 30-60-90 triangle splits into two 30-60-90 triangles each with area $\frac{1}{4} \cdot \frac{1}{4} \cdot \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{32}$, so together they have area $\frac{1}{16}$.  Combining, the whole right triangle has area $\frac{3}{16} + \frac{1}{16} = \frac{1}{4}$.

There are eight of these right triangles, so the whole octagon has area $\boxed{2}$ (since $8 \cdot \frac{1}{4} = 2$).
</think> <answer>2</answer>

