By Cauchy-Schwarz,
\[(1 + 1 + \dots + 1)\left( \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 \right) \ge \left( \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i \right)^2.\]Thus, $n \cdot 144 = 144n \ge 96^2 = (16)(6)^2 = (4 \cdot 3)^2 = 2^6.$  This implies $n \ge 4.$

Also,
\[(1^3 + 1^3 + \dots + 1^3)\left( \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i \right)^2 \ge \left( \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 \right).\]Therefore, $(n)(96)^2 \ge 216 = (6)^3,$ which implies $n \ge 3.$  However, we've already shown $n \ge 4.$

For $n = 4,$ we have
\[(4)(96) = 384 = \left( \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i \right)^2 = 144n.\]Then $(4)(144) = 576 = 216(4) = \left( \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 \right)^2,$ which works.

For $n > 4,$ we get $n \cdot 144 > 576.$  Then $144n > 2^6,$ $144n > (2^2)(3^2),$ $144n > (2^3)(3^3),$ or $144n > (2^4)(3^4).$  But $n \cdot 96 < 96^2 < (2^6)(3^3),$ so $n \cdot 96 < n \cdot 144 < 144n.$  We cannot get the product to be an exact power of three, so no solutions appear for $n > 4.$

Thus, $\boxed{n = 4}$ is the only possible value, and the sum of all possible values of $n$ is $\boxed{4}.$

