Call $B$ the subset of cats having black fur, and let $M$ be the subset of cats that don't catch mice.  We are trying to find $\left | B \cap M \right |$.  From the information given in this problem, we know that $\left | B \right | = 10$, $\left | M \right | = 17$ and $\left | M \cup B \right | = 24$.  From these, we know that $\left | M \triangle B \right | = 24 - 10 -17 + \left | B \cap M \right | = 24 - 27 + \left | B \cap M \right |$, and we also know that $\left | M \triangle B \right | = \left | M \right | + \left | B \right | - 2 \left | B \cap M \right | = 10 + 17 - 2 \left | B \cap M \right |$.  Thus, we have \begin{align*}
24 - 27 + \left | B \cap M \right | &= 10 + 17 - 2 \left | B \cap M \right |\\
\Rightarrow&\quad 3 \left | B \cap M \right | = 20\\
\Rightarrow&\quad \left | B \cap M \right | = \boxed{6}.
\end{align*}
