response:
To begin, we can find the side length of the square to be 4 inches.

[asy]
fill((3,2)--(4,2)--(4,3)--(3,3)--cycle,gray(.7));
draw((0,0)--(0,4)--(4,4)--(4,0)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((0,0)--(4,0),linewidth(1));
draw((0,0)--(4,4),linewidth(1));
draw((1,1)--(2,2)--(3,2)--(4,3)--(3,3)--(3,2)--(3,3),linewidth(1));
draw((3,3)--(4,4),linewidth(1));
label("$A$",(0,4),W);
label("$B$",(4,4),E);
label("$C$",(4,0),E);
label("$D$",(0,0),W);
label("$\text{e}$",(3,2));[/asy]

The four large triangles in the tangram, including the gray piece, have equal area to each other. Let the area of the gray piece be $A$, so each of the other pieces have area $16-2A$ (since the area of the square is 16 square inches). The gray piece and the small square have the same area, so the area of the small square is also $A$. The small triangle and the small square share a side, so a leg of the small triangle is 1. Since the small triangle is isosceles and right, the other leg is also 1. Then by the Pythagorean theorem, the hypotenuse has length $\sqrt{2}$. Thus the height of the grey piece is also $\sqrt{2}$. Therefore the area of the grey piece is $A=\sqrt{2} \cdot \frac{1}{2} =\boxed{\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}$
