\[c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2ab \cos C.\]Rearranging, we get $2ab \cos C = a^2 + b^2 - c^2.$  Squaring both sides, we get
\[4a^2 b^2 \cos^2 C = a^4 - 2a^2 b^2 + b^4 + 2a^2 b^2 - 2c^2 \cdot a^2 - c^2 \cdot b^2 + c^4.\]Then
\begin{align*}
a^4 + b^4 + c^4 &= 2c^2 (a^2 + b^2) + 4a^2 b^2 \cos^2 C \\
&= 2c^2 (a^2 + b^2) + 2a^2 b^2 (1 + \cos^2 C - \cos^2 C - \cos^2 C) \\
&= 2c^2 (a^2 + b^2) + 2a^2 b^2 (1 - 3 \cos^2 C).
\end{align*}Then $1 - 3 \cos^2 C = 1,$ so $\cos^2 C = 0.$  Hence, $\cos C = 0,$ which means that $\angle C = \boxed{90^\circ}.$

