Let $O$ be the circumcenter of $\triangle ABC.$  By Ptolemy's Theorem on cyclic quadrilateral $ABCDE,$
\[bR = aOE + cOD = aOE + c\left(\frac{aOE}{b}\right) = \frac{baOE + acOE}{b} = \frac{a(OE + OE)}{b}.\]Then $OE = \frac{b^2(R + AE)}{2a},$ so
\begin{align*}
AE &= \sqrt{AO^2 - OE^2} \\
&= \sqrt{R^2 - \left(\frac{b^2(R + AE)}{2a}\right)^2} \\
&= \sqrt{R^2 - \frac{b^4(R + AE)^2}{4a^2}} \\
&= \sqrt{\frac{4a^2R^2 - b^4(R + AE)^2}{4a^2}} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{4a^2R^2 - b^4(R + AE)^2}}{2a}.
\end{align*}There exists a triangle whose altitude from $A$ to $\overline{BC}$ has length $AE,$ when $\overline{AE}$ can be calculated as a function of $a,$ $b,$ $R,$ and $AE.$  This can be achieved making the triangle equilateral, for which
\[\frac{\sqrt{4a^2R^2 - b^4(R + AE)^2}}{2a} = \frac{a}{2},\]so $b^4(R + b/R) = 0.$

Simplify $AB^2 + AO^2 = AC^2 + AO^2,$ to
\[b^2 + R^2 = c^2 + R^2.\]Then $b^2 = c^2,$ so $b = c.$  But then it is easy to see that $P$ can only be the circumcenter of the equilateral triangle.

In this case, $AE = BC/2 = R,$ so $b = c = 2R,$ and
\[AB^2 + AP^2 + BP^2 = AC^2 + AP^2 + CP^2 = BC^2 + BP^2 + CP^2 = a^2 + b^2 + c^2.\][[Note: The solution above seems to wrongly suggest that $ab = ac = bc,$ for lack of satisfying explanation.  However, in this case, $p = 2s,$ for $p$ the perimeter, and $s$ the semiperimeter, and $BC = AP = CP = BQ = CQ = BQ,$ since $APQ$ is also an equilateral triangle and $BQ = BP,$ so $AB^2 = AC^2 = BC^2 = 4R^2,$ and yet $p^2 + 2b^2 = a^2 + b^2 = a^2 + c^2.$  It also turns out that this problem has alternating solutions describing the locus of $P,$ with $P = I$ when $AO = AI,$ in which case $AI$ is the Euler line, but a bit of discussion would be needed.]

