\[(\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}) \cdot (\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}) = \mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{b}.\]This expands as
\[\mathbf{c} \cdot \mathbf{c} + 2(\mathbf{c} \cdot (\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a})) +
\mathbf{(c} \times \mathbf{a)} \cdot (\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}) = \mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{b}.\]Since $\mathbf{c}$ and $\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}$ are orthogonal, the middle term vanishes.  We also have $\mathbf{c} \cdot \mathbf{c} = \|\mathbf{c}\|^2 = \frac{4}{7}$ and $\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{b} = \|\mathbf{b}\|^2 = 1,$ so we obtain
\[\frac{4}{7} + \|\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}\|^2 = 1.\]Rearranging gives
\[\|\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}\|^2 = \frac{3}{7}.\]But
\[\|\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}\| = \|\mathbf{c}\| \|\mathbf{a}\| \sin \theta = \frac{2}{\sqrt{7}},\]where $\theta$ is the angle between $\mathbf{c}$ and $\mathbf{a}.$  Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\sin \theta &= \frac{\|\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}\|}{\|\mathbf{c}\| \|\mathbf{a}\|} \\
&= \frac{\frac{2}{\sqrt{7}}}{1 \cdot \frac{2}{\sqrt{7}}} \\
&= 1,
\end{align*}so $\theta = \boxed{90^\circ}.$

