The logarithm function is only defined for positive arguments, so we require $\sin x > 0.$ The sine function is positive in the open interval $\left( 0, \pi \right),$ but sine has a period of $2\pi,$ and $\sin x > 0$ in each interval of the form $\left(2 \pi k, 2 \pi k +\pi \right),$ for all integers $k.$ Thus $\log_2 \sin x$ is defined for $x$ in these intervals.

The sine function has a maximum value of 1, so $\sin x \le 1.$ Since $\log_2 1 = 0,$ the range of the function $\log_2 (\sin x)$ for $0 < \sin x \le 1$ is $(-\infty,0].$ Thus, the range of the function $\sqrt{\log_2 (\sin x)}$ is $(-\infty,0],$ and the number of integers in its range is $\boxed{1}.$

